    /// List Content. Returns a paginated list of content. Every request must scope what it
    /// covers: supply at least one of `creator_id`, `creator_username`, `search`, `brand_id`,
    /// or `external_id`, otherwise the request is rejected with `VALIDATION_ERROR`. By default
    /// results are ordered by `posted_at` descending (most recently posted first); override
    /// with `order_by`. Every response echoes the effective defaults in `applied_filters` (the
    /// resolved date window, `page`, `page_size`, and `brand_id`), so an empty `results` set
    /// can be explained. ### Dates Every request covers a time window. Four parameters define
    /// it. | Parameter | Type | Defines | |---|---|---| | `posted_after` | timestamp | Start of
    /// the window | | `posted_within_hours` | integer, 1 to 8760 | Start of the window, counted
    /// back from the current time | | `posted_within_days` | integer, 1 to 365 | Start of the
    /// window, counted back from the current time | | `posted_before` | timestamp | End of the
    /// window | A request with no date parameter covers the last 7 days. `applied_filters`
    /// reports this as `posted_within_days: 7`. Three parameters define the start of the
    /// window, so a request may carry more than one. Such a request is valid. The API applies
    /// the first parameter present in this order and ignores the rest: 1. `posted_after` 2.
    /// `posted_within_hours` 3. `posted_within_days` `applied_filters` contains only the
    /// parameter that was applied, so it identifies the window that produced a response.
    /// `posted_before` defines the end of the window and combines with any of the three. On its
    /// own it covers the 366 days before it. With `posted_within_hours` or `posted_within_days`
    /// the window is counted back from `posted_before` instead of from the current time, so
    /// `posted_before=2026-09-01T00:00:00Z` with `posted_within_hours=24` covers
    /// `2026-08-31T00:00:00Z` to `2026-09-01T00:00:00Z`. Two limits apply to every window.
    /// `posted_after` and `posted_before` may be at most 366 days apart, and a longer period is
    /// read as consecutive requests. Content posted before 2025-01-01 is not available. A value
    /// outside the ranges in the table returns `VALIDATION_ERROR`. Such values are rejected,
    /// not clamped: `posted_within_days=400` is an error rather than 365. Results are
    /// paginated: `page` defaults to `1` and `page_size` to `30`, which is also the maximum
    /// (larger values are reduced to `30`). `item_count` is the number of rows in the returned
    /// page, not the size of the whole match set, and `next` is non-null while further pages
    /// remain. For the total number of matching posts, call `content.statistics` with the same
    /// query and read `post_count`. When `brand_id` is omitted, the brand-specific fields
    /// (`brand_id`, `is_owned`, `is_tagged`, `is_influencer`, `mention_type`,
    /// `net_promoter_score`) are returned as `null`, and the brand-only filters and sorts
    /// return `BRAND_ID_REQUIRED`. Supply `brand_id` (one or more values, comma-separated) to
    /// populate those fields and enable the brand-only filters. Each row's `id` is the value to
    /// use for `content.details` and for the `comments.list` `content_id`.
